== Limitations

As described above, the project has resulted in our successfully producing a harmonized @DTM of the Rhine catchment area. Furthermore, the automated pipeline for processing and analysis has been completed and tested, resulting in the evaluation of data quality between bordering regions to determine the quality of the product.

However, despite these successes there are limitations to the achievements of the project. The first of which is the scope of the project itself. While not origionally intended, the project group consisted of only three, instead of six, students for a period of ten weeks. Within this timeframe, the aquisition, harmonisation, border analysis, evaluation, project management, and reporting had to be completed. As discussed during meetings and feedback opportunities during the project, the efforts of all three members were required at the same time for each of these topics. For example, the harmonisation in week 5 came to a complete stop due to the required reporting for that week. As a result, several initially intended parts of the scope were cut, including point cloud @DTM re-extraction, automated artifact correction, and more extensive validation of the catchment area @DTM.

The available time and researches were further reduced by previously identified risks (@Risk_Assessment_Table). During week six the processing server was unavailable due to maintenance which was not communicated to the users. During this time, coding and documentation continued, but no testing or processing was possible, losing us time. Furthermore, personal circumstances affected the availability of two team members, one during week 8 and the other during week 9. These events occurred during the final stages of analysis and reporting, leaving little time for addressing final bug fixes. However, through the specified mitigation, the impact was minimized as best as possible.

A second limitation is the reliance on external datasets. The quality of the end product is determined by the sum of its parts. Throughout the analysis it became clear that most artifacts are the result of temporal, spatial and processing differences between datasets, and not neccesarily the processing pipeline. In several cases, large artifacts such as extrapolation data, or misaligned tiles caused large disagreements in the elevation data. In other cases, the exact cause could not be estabilished due to the unavailability of data about processing methodology, or point cloud data to evaluate the raw data for clues.

Another limitation is that the scope of the project was reduced to focus primarily on @DTM:pls instead of point clouds. While it significantly reduced the total workload, it did prevent evaluating raw instead of processed elevation data. The raw data should in theory contain fewer processing differences such as ground filtering or water handling. Furthermore, using one unified processing method would have eliminated the need for a water mask.

The water mask itself also introduces limitations. While not an originally intended element of the pipeline, the analysis proved that water was a consistent source of large disagreements and would need to be filtered out for any meaningful analysis to be carried out. However, due to the impromptu nature of this feature, it makes assumptions about water such as having small elevation changes, and requires a course, incomplete existing mask to function. So while the analysis benefitted greatly from the feature, it will require more time and effort to perfect, should this project be continued.

Several limitations pertaining to the border analysis itself also exist. The comparisons evaluate agreement based on the quality of the overlap, instead of their absolute accuracy. Accounting for the dataset accuracy and combining it with the border differences would give a better idea of the accuracy of the @DTM, however due to the lack such metadata, it is not possible to determine this accuracy. However, the approach does highlight why current approaches such as Mapterhorn fail meet @INSPIRE requirements by smoothing over the data even when inappropriate.

Finally, a number of artifact categories are based on interpretation of the data, instead of numerical proof. While in many cases interpretation allows the cause of a difference to be determined based on facts and data, this is not always possible. Some encountered artifacts remain hypotheses. Verifying wether the interpretations are correct would require processing of metadata, and classified point cloud data for all regions. Such data was not widely available, or within the scope and temporal constraints of the project.

Despite all these limitations and setbacks, the project does demonstrate that a harmonized cross-border @DTM can be constructed in an automated manner from heterogeneous datasets. Furthermore, the analysis shows that many of the inconsistencies can be traced back to source data inconsistencies, rather than the pipeline, which can in most cases be solved through reprocessing of the data using point clouds. The identified limitations therefore represent both challenges for future works, and opportunities for improving cross-border interoperability for elevation datasets in Europe.
